## litellm

- **What we redistribute:** the LiteLLM proxy, installed from the PyPI wheel
  (`litellm[proxy]`) into `ghcr.io/digithings-ai/litellm` by
  [`Dockerfile.litellm`](Dockerfile.litellm).
- **Version:** `1.72.6` (exact pin; not a range).
- **Source:** the Python package repository, PyPI. We do **not** redistribute
  BerriAI's container image. See
  [docs/architecture/litellm-pypi-image.md](docs/architecture/litellm-pypi-image.md).
- **Copyright:** `Copyright (c) 2023 Berri AI`

### The licence is split, and only half of it is MIT

This is the part that matters, so it is quoted rather than summarised. From
upstream's `LICENSE`:

> Portions of this software are licensed as follows:
>
> * All content that resides under the "enterprise/" directory of this repository,
>   if that directory exists, is licensed under the license defined in
>   "enterprise/LICENSE".
> * Content outside of the above mentioned directories or restrictions above is
>   available under the MIT license as defined below.

Everything we redistribute is the second half: content **outside** `enterprise/`.
The MIT text applies to it in full.

What that means in practice, and why the image is built the way it is: the MIT grant
covers the MIT tree, and `enterprise/LICENSE.md` grants production use of the
proprietary tree only to subscribers holding an Enterprise seat licence, with an
explicit development-and-testing carve-out. So a local stack is licensed and a
production gateway is not. `Dockerfile.litellm` asserts at build time that no
`enterprise/` directory and no `litellm-enterprise` distribution is present, which is
what keeps the MIT grant the only one that applies to the image we publish.

